
[38;5;231m⏺[39m That settles the scope question, and it's worth being blunt about what it means: [1mthere is nothing to notify about yet.
[0m  Tasks don't exist, there's no storage, no identity, and nothing that observes changes. A notifications system built
  here would be a delivery pipeline with no events flowing into it.

  So this decomposes into at least three sub-projects, and notifications is the last one:

  1. [1mTasks + persistence[0m — a task model, create/edit/complete, something that survives a reload. Nothing else is
     possible without this.
  2. [1mA source of change worth reporting[0m — whatever makes a task change when you aren't looking. This is the one that
     actually determines the architecture.
  3. [1mNotifications[0m — subscription ("tasks they care about"), an event log, read/unread state, and delivery.

  I'd brainstorm #1 properly and design it with #3 in mind, rather than spec notifications now. But before I even
  recommend that ordering, one question decides the whole shape — including whether #1 is a weekend or a month.

  [1mThe fork:[0m what makes a task change when the user isn't the one changing it? Right now the user is the only actor, so
  "notify me when a task changes" means "notify me about the thing I just did." Something has to change tasks outside
  the user's attention, and the candidates cost wildly different amounts.

  - [1mTime[0m — due dates, reminders, overdue transitions. Buys real notifications with zero server: it all runs client-side
    against stored tasks. Costs almost nothing beyond the task model you need regardless. Rules out nothing — you can
    add people later.
  - [1mOther people[0m — shared or assigned tasks, collaborators editing what you watch. Buys the notifications you probably
    pictured. Costs accounts, auth, a server, a database, and a sync or polling story — that's the bulk of the project,
    and notifications become a small part of it. Expensive to retrofit [3maway from[0m if it turns out you didn't need it.
  - [1mExternal systems[0m — imports, a calendar sync, an integration writing tasks in the background. Buys change-without-you
    without full multi-user. Costs a backend anyway, plus whatever you're integrating with.

  [1mMy recommendation: time.[0m It's the only option that produces a working, genuinely useful notification on top of a task
  model you have to build anyway, and the durable parts — a notification record, read/unread, a subscription notion —
  carry over unchanged if you add people later. The delivery channel is the part you'd revisit, and that's the cheap
  part. Going multi-user first means months before the first notification fires.

